.TH BIGFLOATNORMALIZE 3 "2026-09-21" "BigNums" "Library Functions Manual"
.nr HY 0
.ds AD l
.SH NAME
bigFloatNormalize, bigFloatTruncate \- normalize or reduce a big float mantissa
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include \(dqbignums.h\(dq
.sp
.BI "int bigFloatNormalize(BigFloat *" "x" ");"
.sp
.BI "void bigFloatTruncate(BigFloat *" "x" ", int " "target_limbs" ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bigFloatNormalize()
removes leading zero limbs, then left-shifts the mantissa until the most
significant limb has bit 31 set.  It reduces exp by the shift count to
preserve the value.  A zero mantissa becomes canonical positive zero.
.PP
.B bigFloatTruncate()
clamps target_limbs below 1 to 1.  If the mantissa is larger than the target,
it discards low whole limbs, increases exp by 32 times the discarded limb
count, and normalizes.  Otherwise it does nothing.
Truncation loses low bits; it does not round to nearest or preserve the exact
value.  This is a public helper, also used by iterative routines.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Normalization returns 0 or INT_MAX if the mantissa cannot be shifted.
Truncation returns no value and does not report normalization failures.
.SH BUGS
Normalization inherits the conservative capacity check of
.BR bigIntShiftLeft (3).
Exponent adjustment is not checked for signed overflow.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bigFloatReciprocal (3),
.BR bignums (3),
.BR bignums (7)
